o configure your VS Code settings to use `goimport-reviser` for formatting your Go code. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do this:\n\n### Step 1: Install `goimport-reviser`\n\nFirst, you need to install `goimport-reviser` if you haven't already. You can do this by running the following command:\n\n```bash\ngo get -u github.com/incu6us/goimports-reviser/v2\n```\n\n### Step 2: Configure VS Code Settings\n\n1. **Open VS Code Settings**: Open the Command Palette in VS Code (use `Ctrl+Shift+P` on Windows/Linux or `Cmd+Shift+P` on macOS), then type `Preferences: Open Settings (JSON)` and select it. This will open your `settings.json` file.\n\n2. **Update Go Formatting Tool**: In the `settings.json` file, you need to specify `goimport-reviser` as the formatting tool. Add or modify the following lines:\n\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"go.formatTool\": \"goimports-reviser\",\n        \"go.alternateTools\": {\n            \"goimports\": \"goimports-reviser\"\n        },\n        \"[go]\": {\n            \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n            \"editor.codeActionsOnSave\": {\n                \"source.organizeImports\": true\n            }\n        },\n    }\n    ```\n\n    - `\"go.formatTool\": \"goimports-reviser\"` tells VS Code to use `goimport-reviser` for formatting.\n    - `\"go.alternateTools\": {\"goimports\": \"goimports-reviser\"}` ensures that when VS Code tries to use `goimports`, it will use `goimport-reviser` instead.\n    - The `[go]` settings ensure that your Go code is formatted on save and imports are organized.\n\n3. **Save the Settings File**: After making these changes, save the `settings.json` file.\n\n### Step 3: Test the Configuration\n\nTo test the configuration, open a Go file in VS Code and try editing and saving it. The `goimport-reviser` should automatically format your code and organize imports when you save the file.\n\n### Additional Notes\n\n- Ensure that `goimport-reviser` is installed in a location that is in your system's PATH, so VS Code can access it.\n- You may need to restart VS Code for the changes to take effect.\n- If you encounter any issues, double-check the paths and settings, and make sure that `goimport-reviser` is correctly installed and accessible from the command line.\n\nThis should set up `goimport-reviser` as your default import formatting tool in VS Code for Go development.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1705696050.61252,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2eb0c-939f-4c64-9590-8a3997c6461f","source_parent_id":"f38a66a6-a31f-4f5e-b16e-d663b54e14e1","source_record_index":2,"text":"how can I use goimport-reviser with vscode instead of goimports","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["how can I use goimport-reviser with vscode instead of goimports"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"The task concerns import tooling in VS Code, specifically goimport-reviser and goimports.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"specificity":[{"category":"specificity","evidence_spans":["how can I use goimport-reviser with vscode instead of goimports"],"item_id":"specificity_1","normalized_text":"Use goimport-reviser with VS Code in place of goimports.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"verification":[]},"identity":{"case_id":"CASE_CE4BF3CB944D","conversation_id":"d2590505-31e2-4e34-be93-8e4fabf8d872","conversation_url":"https://chat.openai.com/share/d2590505-31e2-4e34-be93-8e4fabf8d872","outcome_class":"PN","pr_conversation_correspondence":"yes","pr_number":338,"pr_url":"https://github.com/pokt-network/poktroll/pull/338","repository":"pokt-network/poktroll"},"methodology_version":"dir-tfg-v2","primary_repository_cutoff":"tFG","record_status":"frozen","schema_version":"stage-e-frozen-conversation-v1","scientific_provenance":{"eligibility":{"decision":"eligible","manifest_row":{"adjudication_required":"false","authoritative_target_identif